<exercises xml:id="ex-connectedness-compactness" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Exercises related to connectedness and compactness</title>
    <exercisegroup>
    
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Check whether the following statements are true or false.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>
                    The following letters when considered as subspaces of <m>\R^2</m> are pairwise non-homeomorphic.
                    <me>\mathsf{A}\qquad \mathsf{B}\qquad \mathsf{C}</me>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="intersection-of-compact-subspaces">
            <statement correct="no">
                <p>
                    Intersection of two distinct compact subspaces is compact.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Let <m>\R</m> be given a topology generated by <m>\{(a,\infty):a\in\R\}</m>. Note that <m>A\subseteq\R</m> is compact in this topology if the set <m>A</m> has a minimum. Thus, if we consider <m>A=[0,1)\cup (3,\infty)</m> and <m>B=[2,\infty)</m> then, both <m>A,B</m> are compact but their intersection <m>A\cap B=(3,\infty)</m> is not compact.
                </p>
                <p>
                    We remark that if the topological space is Hausdorff then the intersection of two compact subspaces is indeed compact.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>
                    The subspace <m>X=[0,1]\cap\Q</m> of <m>\R</m> is not compact.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    If <m>X</m> is a compact subspace of <m>\R</m> then, <m>X</m> must be closed because <m>\R</m> is a Hausdorff space.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="GL-is-not-connected">
            <statement correct="no">
                <p>
                    For any <m>n\geq 1</m>, the general linear group <m>{\rm GL}(n,\R)</m> is connected.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    One may consider <m>{\rm GL}(1,\R)</m>. Alternatively one may consider the continuous function <m>\det\colon{\rm GL}(n,\R)\to{\rm GL}(1,\R)</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="orthogonal-group-is-compact">
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>
                    The orthogonal group <m>\Or(n,\R)</m> is compact.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    A subset <m>K</m> of <m>\R^{n^2}</m> is compact if and only if <m>K</m> is closed and bounded. Therefore, it is enough to show that <m>\Or(n,\R)</m> is closed and bounded. Let <m>A\in\Or(n,\R)</m> and let <m>A^t</m> denote the transpose of the matrix <m>A</m>. We have <m>AA^t=I_n</m>, i.e., for <m>1\leq i,j\leq n</m> we have the following.
                    <me>
                        \sum_{k=1}^n a_{ik}a_{jk}=\delta_{ij}
                    </me>
                    In particular, when <m>i=j</m>, we have <m>\sum_{k=1}^na_{ik}a_{ik}=1</m>. Hence, <m>\lvert a_{ik}\rvert\leq 1</m>, and thus, <m>\Or(n,\R)</m> is bounded.
                    For each <m>1\leq i,j\leq n</m> we define <m>f_{ij}\colon M_n(\R)\to\R</m> given by <m>(a_{rs})\mapsto \sum_{k=1}^n a_{ik}a_{jk}=\delta_{ij}</m>. This is a continuous function. We also get that
                    <me>
                        \Or(n,\R)=\cap_{i\neq j}f^{-1}_{ij}\{0\}\bigcap \cap f^{-1}_{ii}\{1\}
                    </me>
                    Thus, <m>\Or(n,\R)</m> is an intersection of closed subsets and hence <m>\Or(n,\R)</m> is closed.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="special-orthogonal-group-is-compact">
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>
                   The special orthogonal group <m>\SO(n,\R)</m> is compact.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    The special orthogonal group is a closed subset of a compact space, viz., <m>\SO(n,\R)=\det^{-1}\{1\}\subseteq\Or(n,\R)</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement correct="no">
                <p>
                    The following subset of <m>M(n,\R)</m> is a connected subspace.
                    <me>
                        \left\{A\in M(n,\R):A^2=\unit_n\right\}
                    </me>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Use determinant.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="symmetric-matrices-are-path-connected">
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>
                    The following subset of <m>M(n,\R)</m> is a path connected subspace.
                    <me>
                        \left\{A\in M(n,\R):A=A^t\right\}
                    </me>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    The given subset is the set of all symmetric matrices. As it is a linear subspace, it is path connected.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="indiscrete-space-is-path-connected">
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>
                    Any indiscrete topological space is path connected.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                   If <m>X</m> is a topological space with indiscrete topology then, <em>any</em> function <m>f\colon [0,1]\to X</m> is continuous.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="circle-not-homeomorphic-to-subset-of-reals">
            <statement correct="no">
                <p>
                    The circle <m>\mathbb{S}^1</m> is homeomorphic to a subset of <m>\R</m>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    If <m>f\colon\mathbb{S}^1\to X</m> is such a homeomorphism then, <m>X</m> is a compact, connected subspace of <m>\R</m>, i.e., <m>X</m> is a closed, bounded interval. If we remove two endpoints of <m>X</m> then, the new space is connected. However, removing two points from <m>\mathbb{S}^1</m> gives a disconnected space.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="Cantor-set-is-compact">
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>
                    Let <m>C_0=[0,1]</m>, <m>C_1=[0,\tfrac{1}{3}]\cup [\tfrac{2}{3},1]</m>. Iteratively we define
                    <me>
                        C_i=\left\{\tfrac{1}{3}x:x\in C_{i-1}\right\}\bigcup\left\{\tfrac{2}{3}+\tfrac{1}{3}x:x\in C_{i-1}\right\}
                    </me>
                    Thus, we obtain <m>C_{i}</m> by removing the open middle third of each interval appearing in <m>C_{i-1}</m>. Then following set (Cantor set) is compact.
                    <me>\bigcap_iC_i</me>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                The Cantor set is an intersection of closed sets and hence it is closed. Also it is a subset of <m>[0,1]</m> and hence bounded. 
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement correct="no">
                <p>
                    Let <m>X=C[0,1]</m> with sup norm. The closed unit ball is compact in <m>X</m>.
                </p>
                
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                Note that in the sup norm the convergence is uniform. So, one may consider <m>f_n=x^n</m>.
            </feedback>
        </exercise>
        
    </exercisegroup>
</exercises>